      {"t": 0.0, "at": [37.6, -0.98], "label": "New Carthage", "color": "#6d3fb8"},
      {"t": 3.3, "at": [43.9, 4.65], "label": "Rhône crossing", "color": "#2f86d6"},
      {"t": 5.2, "at": [44.7, 7.0], "label": "Alpine pass (debated)", "color": "#7a5a2a", "pulse": true},
      {"t": 6.5, "at": [45.2, 9.0], "label": "Ticinus", "color": "#e8603c"},
      {"t": 7.5, "at": [45.03, 9.6], "label": "Trebia", "color": "#e8603c", "pulse": true}
    ],
    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 0.0,
        "text": "Late spring 218 BC. Hannibal leaves New Carthage with, Polybius says, about 90,000 infantry, 12,000 cavalry and 37 elephants."
      },
      {
        "t": 1.1,
        "text": "North of the Ebro he fights his way through the local tribes and leaves garrisons behind him."
      },
      {
        "t": 2.5,
        "text": "Over the Pyrenees and into Gaul. Consul Publius Scipio, meant to stop him in Spain, sails west from Pisa."
      },
      {
        "t": 3.3,
        "text": "At the Rhône, Gauls hold the far bank. Hannibal sends a force upstream to hit them from behind, and floats the elephants across on rafts."
      },
      {
        "t": 3.6,
        "text": "Scipio lands at Massalia and reaches the crossing three days too late. He sends his army on to Spain and hurries back to Italy."
      },
      {"t": 4.4, "text": "Up into the Alps as winter closes in. Mountain tribes ambush the column in the passes."},
      {
        "t": 5.1,
        "text": "At the top, Hannibal shows his exhausted men the plains of Italy below. The descent is worse: snow, ice and a landslide that has to be cut through."
      },
      {
        "t": 5.7,
        "text": "He reaches the Po valley with about 20,000 infantry and 6,000 cavalry. The crossing took fifteen days."
      },
      {
        "t": 6.5,
        "text": "First clash, a cavalry fight at the Ticinus. Scipio is wounded; legend says his teenage son, the future Africanus, saved him."
      },
      {"t": 7.4, "text": "December: Hannibal wins at the Trebia, and the Gauls of northern Italy flock to join him."}
    ]
  }
}
